Introduction 

Study Buddy is a student planner website designed to help users manage tasks, stay organised, and maintain focus. Our team collaborated to develop various features; each member responsible for a specific section. This report summarizes the development, testing, and improvements made to the site. 

1. Project Overview 

Study Buddy was designed as a practical tool for students to organize their study schedules, track tasks, and boost productivity. Our team divided the work to ensure a focused approach: 

Suraiya: Developed the homepage and To-Do List page. 
Sovanndevid: Created the Pomodoro Timer and enhanced the header and footer. 
Manjil: Developed the Weekly Timetable feature. 
Shovana: Created the Motivational Quotes and Countdown Timer pages. 
This collaborative approach allowed us to maintain consistency in design while ensuring that each page was well-developed. 

2. Initial Setup and Design 

The initial setup of the Study Buddy website is directed to a more clean, modular structure across the domain for consistent development and collaboration. Our domain structure was agreed on separating each individual component into subfolder for better clarity and maintainability. 

In the early stages, our site experimented with  a Dark Mode toggle, but due to technical issues and inconsistent behaviour, we removed it. Our focus then shifted to ensuring consistent design across all pages, including a well-structured navigation bar and footer. The navigation bar is now sticky, ensuring easy access, while the footer remains fixed without overlapping content. 

Each team member established the assigned feature relying on a share ideas/design. This will enable a unform typography, styling, spacing and colour themes.  

3. Design and Code Structure 

To maintain a clean, organized codebase, we separated HTML, CSS, and JavaScript files for each feature. The site follows a consistent layout, with a shared header and footer for easy navigation. We focused on a simple, user-friendly interface with responsive design across all devices. 

HTML: Organized with separate files for each page (Homepage, To-Do List, Pomodoro Timer, Weekly Timetable, Quotes, Countdown Timer). 
CSS: Separate style sheets for each feature, ensuring easy updates and consistent design. 
JavaScript: Interactive elements like task management (To-Do List) and timer functionalities were developed using clean, modular code. 
The pattern of the site is developed with a clear separation between the features. Features such as To-Do-List, Countdown, Pomodoro Timer, and Motivation Quote were designed as its own section of the site with its own set of the interaction include:   
 
To Do List – A built-in task input features enable individual to add, remove and edit task. It quick and easy designed made to support the user’s task management needs.  
Countdown – A simple and clean countdown timer allow users to use for specific tasks/activities. Its fun and color-coded feedback allow for a better visualisation and engagements.  
Pomodoro Timers – A more focused timer where its alternate between session time and break time for better productivity. Its clear visual feedback such as progress ring and numeric countdown made to assist users to manage their time effectively.  
Motivational Quotes – A feature that randomly displays inspiring/meaningful quotes to help users stay motivated during their session. The page is designed to provide encouragements and to help boost the individual’s positivity and focus.   
Weekly Timetable – A weekly planner that offer customisation with editable time slots and auto saving function, result in minimise any procrastination for a task and encourage time management. The option to send over the weekly timetable and a reminder to peers add additional value to the overall pages. 
 
Navigation Flow:   
To ensure a smooth transition between features, the navigation is designed to be simple and efficient with minimal clicks. A sticky header bar at the top provides a quick access to all the features alongside the minimal footers that can be seen across the sites. Additionally, each feature is placed in its own interactive cards with consistent styling.  
 

4. Testing Process 

We used Guerrilla Testing among our four team members to ensure the site was functional, accessible, and user-friendly. Our testing process included: 

Visual Testing: Checking layout, fonts, and spacing for consistent design. 
Functionality Testing: Verifying button interactions, task inputs, and timer operations. 
Accessibility Testing: Running lighthouse tools to ensure appropriate colour contrast, alt text for images, and proper keyboard navigation. 
Responsive Testing: Testing the site on different devices (desktop, tablet, mobile) to ensure a consistent experience. 
Peer Review: Team members reviewed each other’s pages, providing direct feedback for improvements. 
These tests guided offer a lot of value in terms of domain refinements, resulting in more stale, inclusive, and user-friendly products. 

5. Test Results and Analysis 

Guerrilla Testing Insight: 

We presented the finalised website to our four team members to perform internal guerrilla testing based on our earlier user hypotheses.  Individual are assigned to conducts the tasks that aligned with the core features while observing the overall usability and interaction flow of the site.  
These quick conducts has enabled us to identify important usability issues at every stage of our developments, allowing for a quick iteration and targeted improvements across the domain.  

The testing process highlighted a few. key issues: 

Dark Mode Toggle: Initially implemented but removed due to inconsistent behaviour across pages. 
Footer Overlap: Fixed by standardizing footer position and making it sticky for consistent display. 
Responsive Design: Adjusted layout for a better view on smaller devices, ensuring elements do not overlap. 
Accessibility: Enhanced by improving text contrast, alt text for images, and consistent font sizes. 
Button Alignment and Sizing: Unified all buttons using a share style sheet. 
Based on the test results, we made targeted improvements to ensure a smoother user experience. 

6. Improvements Made 

Our team made several enhancements based on the test results: 

Standardised Layout: Improved button and text alignment for a consistent look. 
Footer and Navigation Bar: Adjusted for better functionality without overlapping content. 
Accessibility Enhancements: Improved color contrast and added alt text for images. 
Code Optimization: CSS and JavaScript were separated from HTML, making the code easier to manage. 
Responsive Design: Layout adjusted to ensure proper display across devices. 
6. Final Analysis 

Working on Study Buddy taught us the importance of clean code, user-friendly design, and effective teamwork. We learned to identify and resolve design and functionality issues quickly through peer review and guerrilla testing. Our focus on modular code and clean design ensured that the site remains easy to update and maintain. 

Despite challenges with the Dark Mode toggle, we maintained a consistent, accessible design and ensured all key features were functional. Our team’s collaboration and consistent feedback allowed us to create a practical tool that can genuinely help students organise their study life. 

7. Conclusion 

Study Buddy is now a complete, functional, and accessible student planner website. It allows users to manage tasks, track study time, and stay motivated. The site is live on Bitbucket, meeting our initial goals and providing a practical tool for students. 

